  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA deep trough is in place across the Great Basin, with \nsoutheast flow building across the central High Plains. Our area\nis still under the influence of westerly flow aloft, with an \nembedded shortwave in SW flow to the south. After a more \norganized system moves through the region through Tuesday, \nsouthwest flow develops and persists at least through the end of\nthe week and potentially beyond the weekend (some variance \nremains in clusters between ridging and SW by this weekend). The\ngeneral trend should be for seasonal temperatures to warm \nslightly from seasonal highs in the 40s-lower 50s to slightly \nabove average highs in the mid 50s-lower 60s. SW flow aloft \ninherently brings the potential for additional chances for \nprecipitation. However, due to the weak nature of the upper flow\nin the Plains and variance in guidance resolving any embedded \nwaves, confidence in measurable precipitation/impacts is low \nbeyond Wednesday. Another progressive shortwave passes through \nWednesday, bringing likely chances for another wetting rain over\nwest central Minnesota. Otherwise, a mostly dry pattern with \nslight warming trend through Friday.\n \n...Rain Tonight into Tuesday...\n\nA stronger mid level wave is organizing into a hybrid type \nsystem with large scale ascent increasing across our CWA south \nto north late this afternoon through Tuesday. Initially drier \nair in northeast boundary layer flow will delay measurable \nrainfall, deeper moisture will support 700mb deformation banding\nof rainfall across the southern zones tonight. Stronger areas \nof frontogenesis will tend to be transient making coverage of \nhigher totals 0.5\"+ limited. Better coverage of rain will tend \nto initially be along and south of US Highway 200, though the \ntrack of the mid/upper low will eventually allow rain to \noverspread northwest/north central MN. Moderate to high \nprobabilities (50-80%) for 0.25\" or more rainfall are shown by \nNBM/HREF across southeast ND and west central MN, with a 30-50% \nchance in similar areas for greater than 0.5\" of rainfall. \nTemperatures and precipitation rates tonight overall do not \nsupport any accumulating snowfall, just novelty flakes mixing \nin. If sufficient banding and dynamic cooling occurs, a light \ndusting on grassy/elevated surfaces cannot be ruled out \n(especially west of the Red River Valley).",
